WSL2 + Ubuntu + GCC Setup
OS Practical 1 – Fork System Call
STEP 1 — Install WSL2 and Ubuntu
This will: Install WSL and Ubuntu on Windows.

Terminal: Windows PowerShell (Run as Administrator)

Command:
wsl --install -d Ubuntu


Restart your computer if prompted.

STEP 2 — Configure WSL2
This will: Update WSL, set WSL2 as default and verify installation.

Terminal: Windows PowerShell (Administrator)

Commands:
wsl --update
wsl --set-default-version 2
wsl --status
wsl --list --verbose


Check: Ubuntu should show VERSION 2.

STEP 3 — Create Ubuntu Account
This will: Create your Linux username and password.

Application: Ubuntu
1. Open Ubuntu from the Windows Start menu.
2. Enter your username.
3. Enter your password.
4. Confirm your password.
Note: Password characters will not appear while typing.

STEP 4 — Update Ubuntu
This will: Update package information and upgrade installed packages.

Terminal: Ubuntu

Commands:
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y



STEP 5 — Install GCC
This will: Install GCC, G++, Make and essential development tools.

Terminal: Ubuntu

Command:
sudo apt install build-essential -y



STEP 6 — Verify GCC and G++
This will: Check whether C and C++ compilers are installed.

Terminal: Ubuntu

Commands:
gcc --version
g++ --version



STEP 7 — Create Practical Folder
This will: Create a folder for Practical 1.

Terminal: Ubuntu

Command:
mkdir -p ~/OS_Practicals/Practical_01_Fork



STEP 8 — Enter Practical Folder
This will: Navigate into the Practical 1 folder and verify your location.

Terminal: Ubuntu

Commands:
cd ~/OS_Practicals/Practical_01_Fork
pwd



STEP 9 — Create C Program
This will: Create practical1.c and automatically save the C program inside it. No separate code editor is required.

Terminal: Ubuntu

Command:
cat > practical1.c <<'EOF'
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/types.h>

int main()
{
    pid_t pid = fork();

    if (pid == -1)
        printf("Fork failed\n");

    else if (pid == 0)
    {
        printf("\nChild Process\n");
        printf("Child PID: %d\n", getpid());
        printf("Parent PID: %d\n", getppid());
    }

    else
    {
        printf("\nParent Process\n");
        printf("Parent PID: %d\n", getpid());
        printf("Child PID: %d\n", pid);
    }

    return 0;
}
EOF


Verify file creation:
ls



STEP 10 — Compile C Program
This will: Compile practical1.c and generate an executable named practical1.

Terminal: Ubuntu (inside Practical_01_Fork)

Command:
gcc practical1.c -o practical1



STEP 11 — Execute C Program
This will: Run the compiled program and display Parent Process and Child Process information.

Terminal: Ubuntu

Command:
./practical1


Example Output:
Parent Process
Parent PID: 3650
Child PID: 3651

Child Process
Child PID: 3651
Parent PID: 3650


Note: PID values and the order of output may change every time you execute the program.
